<GuideLayout slug="waylaid-crates" updated="2026-11-21">
  <h2>What Waylaid Crates are</h2>
  <p>Waylaid Crates drop from monsters and world chests all over Azeroth, and because they can be traded, players also sell them on the auction house. You can't hand in an empty crate: its label asks for a delivery, a <b>bundle</b> of trade goods such as a stack of cloth, herbs or bars. Open the crate with any <b>one</b> of the bundles on its list and it becomes a sealed crate, which is bound to you.</p>
  <p>Hand sealed crates in for <b>Merchant's Favor</b> and reputation:</p>
  <ul class="gems">
    <li><b>Alliance</b>: the Azeroth Commerce Authority at Three Corners in Redridge Mountains.</li>
    <li><b>Horde</b>: Durotar Supply and Logistics, west of the Crossroads in the Barrens.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Your very first crate also starts a one-time quest worth 50 Favor, so the first one is always worth filling.</p>

  <h2>Why the cheapest bundle changes</h2>
  <p>Each crate accepts several different bundles, and each bundle is made of materials with their own auction house prices. One week the cloth bundle is the bargain; the next, everyone has the same idea, cloth goes up, and the herb bundle is half the price. The <a href="/waylaid-crates">Waylaid Crates</a> page prices every bundle of every crate at this hour's prices and tells you which one to buy.</p>

  <h2>Reading the table</h2>
  <ul class="gems">
    <li><b>Per Favor</b> is the cheapest bundle's cost divided by the Favor that crate gives. It's the number to compare crates by: a crate that gives more Favor can be worth a more expensive bundle.</li>
    <li><b>Buy + fill</b> adds the price of the cheapest crate of that kind on the auction house, for when you don't have one and want to buy it too.</li>
    <li>Bundles are priced at their <b>market price</b> (the average of the cheapest 10% on sale) on your faction's auction house or on Blackwater, whichever is cheaper. If a house doesn't have enough units listed to make the whole bundle, it's skipped for that bundle.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="tip">If you've unlocked the Legacy perk that adds Favor to turn-ins, pick its bonus at the top of the page. It changes the Favor of every crate, and with it the gold per Favor, so the best crate can change too.</p>

  <h2>A cheap Favor routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Keep the crates you loot instead of vendoring them.</li>
    <li>Before you head to the auction house, open the Waylaid Crates page and pick your faction's house.</li>
    <li>For each crate you hold, buy the materials for the cheapest bundle shown. Buy them on the house the page names.</li>
    <li>If one crate's <b>Buy + fill</b> is well below the others' <b>Per Favor</b>, buy a few extra crates of that kind.</li>
    <li>Fill and hand them all in on one trip.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Fill it with what you have</h2>
  <p>If you gather or craft, you may already carry a bundle. Its cost is still what those materials would sell for: filling a crate with ore you could sell for 2 gold is a 2-gold crate. Compare that with the cheapest bundle on the page before you decide.</p>
  <p>The crate and bundle lists come from Blizzard's game data and are refreshed every 25 days; the prices every hour.</p>
</GuideLayout>
